> []($block.attrs("author-note"))
> It is no secret to most people that know me that I generally despise LLMs and other
> commercialized generative models (image/music/video/etc) with a deep
> burning passion. I've written some of my thoughts [here](https://purpleshirt.dev/blog/generative-models-and-the-death-of-creativity/),
> but I think it's important to delineate again between what I hate (LLM powered code
> assistants, useless summary tools, "agents" and their various ilk, and "art" generators)
> and what I don't (machine learning tools with actual applications, like translation
> software and search augmentation that **includes sources**).
>
> So in the spirit of [one of my favorite articles of all time](https://ludic.mataroa.blog/blog/i-will-fucking-piledrive-you-if-you-mention-ai-again/),
> I'm gonna get real rant-y with it.

[]($section)
## Timmy's Tall Tale
A few months ago, I was on the bus to Amsterdam Centraal and overheard a conversation
between a couple of Australian tourists sitting behind me. It was a kid who looked like he
was about 10-11 and his dad, and the kid - completely unprompted I might add - says to his
dad: "You make 27 thousand dollars a year."

His father, somewhat bemused, reasonably responds: "What? Where did you get that from?"

This poor child answers back "From Meta AI" (which, sidenote, how fucking dystopian
corporate is it that they called it "Meta AI?" I mean, the weird anthropomorphized ones
aren't really any better, but still). Now, for context, being an American and having
woefully little context on earnings numbers outside my own country or the one I'm living
in, I went and looked up the median income in Australia.

[According to the Australian Government](https://www.abs.gov.au/statistics/labour/earnings-and-working-conditions/employee-earnings/latest-release),
it's ~$AUD 1400 a week, which comes out to just under **$AUD 73,000 a year**. Given that these are
tourists halfway around the world, I think it's safe to assume that that number is
probably a low ballpark.

So, and I cannot emphasize this enough, ***what the fuck man?***

Where the fuck did Meta's training data pull that number from? Ok, sure, there's an
argument to be made that little Timmy (I've decided to call him Timmy) doesn't know
precisely what his dad does and just said something like "what does
*[insert-generic-job-here]* make?" But even still, he has now been confidently told some
absolutely wild outlandish claim by Mr. AI (tm).

"But wait!" I hear an AI bro cry. "Fake information is all over the internet! He could
have found that BS number just by normal googling too!"

Ah, but you see, this would have been a *learning* opportunity for littly Timmy. Because
in all likelihood, he would have found multiple numbers from multiple sources, and then oh
do the cogs go a-whirling. "Why does reddit say one number and this news article say another
number?" he would have cried, falling to his knees in the bus. "Father, I must learn to
think critically about what seems the most accurate, or perchance I shall simply ask you
and know the answer from the source!"

But nah. Instead the magic robot box said "Here kid, I definitely know what I'm
talking about and the fact that I'm presented like a neat little friend to chat with makes
it significantly less likely that you'll question what I say."

> []($block.attrs("author-note"))
> It is fair to say that in all likelihood Timmy would actually have just taken the number
> from reddit and been done with it, but in our little pretend world here we're going to
> assume that Timmy is a curious lad who learned to cross-reference things.

[]($section)
## Hey, Get The Fuck Out of My Life
A month or so after Timmy, when going to update an app on my phone, I was presented with this:
[What the cinnamon toast fuck is this?]($image.asset('sort-life-screenshot.jpg').size(800,0))

If you're anything like me, this makes you sick to your stomach. It caused something
visceral enough in me to start thinking about this blog post.

2025 has been a heavily mixed bag of a year for me, something I plan to write about near
Christmas, but for various reasons (many self-inflicted, some outside my control, and the
rest just life being life) one of my main emotions this year, or at least the one I've
remembered the most, is loneliness. There's no "woe is me" coming here, it's just how
things have shaken out.

So when I saw this advertisement, essentially screaming at me "your life really is messed
up! The only recourse to your sad existence is our slop generator!" (embellishment mine),
it upest me. Deeply. It seemed to worm its way into my head, bringing all of those
terrible, awful feelings of loneliness and worthlessness and despair to the surface in an
instant. Because if my life really does need an "action plan" (side note: gagged in my
mouth even quoting that), maybe there is something truly deeply wrong.

But worry not! OpenAI has the answer to all my problems! Their magic black box will help
me get my worthless meatbag life sorted out! It will get me to that "action plan" (again,
gag)! My measly existence may be worth something after all! Provided, of course, that I
pay them a small monthly fee so that their stupid text predictor continues to work, which
I'm sure they'll try to sell me on off the rip, forever locking me in to dependence. Good
thing they put that little "In-App Purchases" warning that you definitely don't have to
squint to see /s.

I'm being dramatic on purpose here, of course. It may shock you to learn that I did not,
in fact, "sort out my life with ChatGPT." Instead, I did what our species has done for
hundreds of thousands of years and sat down and tried to think about my problems and how
to solve them (or let them go, in some cases). Because as it turns out, the mushy wrinkly
ball that's the product of billions of years of evolution beneath my thick skull is pretty
fucking good at doing that. And it doesn't require that I give money to something that
definitely could not give less of a shit about my well being. If I want to pay for that, I
will give money to another real, physical human person who does give a shit, and has spent
their life & career doing so for others (therapy good!).

Anyway, fuck off with your stomach churning advertisements Altman.

