Last week, I was at my normal cafe, working remotely, when the [recent Cloudflare
debacle](https://blog.cloudflare.com/18-november-2025-outage/) happened. This largely did
not matter to me other than being a minor annoyance for a couple of hours, but the woman
at the table next to me turned to me and asked me if ChatGPT was down for me as well. This
turned out to be a common refrain, and I heard a smattering of other versions throughout
the cafe as well. I explained to her that no, it was just a Cloudflare outage. Then tried
(poorly) to explain what Cloudflare is, then tried to explain what DNS is (at this point I
had assumed it to be a DNS problem on Cloudflare's end), eventually circling back around
to "so yeah, you can't reach ChatGPT because a different tech giant is currently shitting
itself."

> []($block.attrs("author-note"))
> Before I go any further on this section of the rant, I'd like to quickly clarify that
> this woman is another regular at this cafe, who I have talked to before, and is a lovely
> person and extremely smart. I mean this with no ill intent towards her at all, it was
> simply a good narrative framing of my wrath.

When I was done, she made an offhand comment about how she wasn't sure how she'd get any
more work done today without ChatGPT, half-joking I think. And I just have to say: really?
Am I just yelling onto deaf ears? Is this really such a vital technology for others that
it's that critical to doing... anything???

Look, I'm all for using tools that make your job easier. Word processers fix your stupid
typos. Dictation systems (which often run on *useful* forms of machine learning!) make
note taking and stream-of-thought capture trivial. And a million other examples. But those
things are all what I would describe as **augmentative** thinking tools. The human is
still the one who has to produce the actual idea, then express it themselves. LLMs take
away most of this. Sure, there's an argument to be made that there's still human input at
the prompt stage, but crucially *that's where it seems to end.* Gone is the refinement
necessary when trying to express that set of neuron signals as something that other people
can understand, gone is the care and craft that contributes to making things. Instead, a
vauge idea can slop out something that looks good on the surface, but provides nothing of
any actual nuance or interest as soon as you peel away veneer of word vomit and emojis.

LLMs are, and I will die on this (hopefully not super crazy) hill, ***fundamentally
incapable of producing anything novel.*** Because they cannot, by the nature of how they
work. You need a human to do that. And I think that offloading the
text/image/video/whatever generation to these stupid things immediately invalidates any
reasonable ability of the "creator" to claim novel intent. A word processer can correct
your typos, but it still processes *your words.*

[]($section)
## The Part Where I Rant About Writing Code (Again)
I just want to write code. It's fun. I like making the computer do magic by giving it a
stream of arcane commands and then it shoves around a bunch of electrons and makes the
lights go on and off real fast and I get to clap my hands like a little baby and be entertained.

I just want to write code.

I don't *want* to "manage a fleet of agents." I don't *want* to "be an architecht, not a code
monkey." I don't *want* to "make all my ideas come to life in 1/10th the time."

I just want to write code.

Look, the easy response here is "Well then just do, idiot, no one is *forcing* you to use
these things!" That is, technically, true. No one is forcing me. But every fucking single
mother fucking day, I am inundated with these things. At work, from friends in the
industry, from the forums I otherwise enjoy frequenting, from advertisements that slip through my
many layers of blocks or that I can't avoid out in the world, or any number of other
things that I have no control over. That's life, I get it, I just do my best to filter it
out. But man, is it depressing and grating.

I. Just. Want. To. Write. Code.

It is *rewarding* to master that skill. It feels *good* to get into that flow state, to
fucking ***build*** something, MYSELF, not to manage some nightmare-fuel factory line that
trips over itself at the first sign of actual complexity. I want to be **proud** of the
things that ***I*** make, the things I ***learn***, not show off some god damn
Frankenstein's monster of duct tape that has a veneer of quality and is a festering pile
of rat shit on the inside. Are there good quality projects that have been built with LLM
assistance? You bet, [ghostty](https://github.com/ghostty-org/ghostty) is the first that
comes to mind. But the much greater (or maybe just much louder) set of stuff I've
encountered, both in OSS and (depressingly) at work, is rat-shit-in-a-suit. It's code
vomit to get the job done, and nothing more. And sometimes it doesn't even get the job
done.

I know that it's not everyone. And I also know that there are lots of extremely competent
engineers, many that I know personally, that use these things in the *right* way, and that
produce just as well-crafted software as they did before the current age of misery. But
the cost is that now I have to interact more and more with the other side of that coin,
where the craft isn't respected. And that, one last time, *fucking blows massive shitty
nards.*

I am not a brilliant engineer. I'm probably pretty average, all things considered,
maybe a bit better at a few things than most people and a bit worse at others, because
that's just how skill works. But I *earned* that being average, and when this bubble
bursts (when, not if), or if all of these things suddenly disappear into a black hole
singularity tomorrow, I can at least be happy that I wouldn't suddenly drop to my knees in
a Wal-Mart as I watched my so-called "skill" drain away.

`/end-rant`
